## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests your ability to combine multiple data structures to solve a real-world analytics challenge: maintaining sliding window aggregations with fast top-K retrieval under tight latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

1. **Circular bucket array** enables O(1) bucketing and automatic expiration of old events
2. **Hashmap aggregation** provides O(1) lookup and update for per-user totals across categories
3. **Max-heap with lazy deletion** avoids the overhead of rebuilding the heap on every expiration; stale entries are filtered on-demand during `topK`
4. **Lazy expiration** ensures we never scan individual events during query time—only expired buckets trigger cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
class WindowedTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buckets = new Array(W).fill(null).map(() => ({ head: null, tail: null, ts: null }));
    this.totals = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
    this.head = 0;
  }

  insertEvent(ts, userId, category, value) {
    const idx = ts % this.W;
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    const event = { userId, category, value, ts, next: null };
    if (!bucket.head) bucket.head = bucket.tail = event;
    else bucket.tail = bucket.tail.next = event;
    bucket.ts = ts;

    this.totals.set(userId, (this.totals.get(userId) || 0) + value);
    this.heap.push({ total: this.totals.get(userId), userId, ts });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    while (this.buckets[this.head].ts !== null && this.buckets[this.head].ts < cutoff) {
      let event = this.buckets[this.head].head;
      while (event) {
        this.totals.set(event.userId, (this.totals.get(event.userId) || 0) - event.value);
        event = event.next;
      }
      this.buckets[this.head] = { head: null, tail: null, ts: null };
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
    }
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    this.advanceTime(now);
    const result = [];
    let i = 0;
    while (result.length < K && i < this.heap.length) {
      const entry = this.heap[i++];
      if (this.totals.get(entry.userId) === entry.total) {
        result.push(entry.userId);
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- **Per-category top-K**: Maintain separate totals per `(userId, category)` and separate heaps for each category
- **Bounded memory**: Add a periodic rebuild phase or use LRU eviction for rarely-active user_ids
- **Distributed extension**: Partition by user_id hash across nodes, aggregate partial results

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of DAG longest-path DP and cycle detection using topological sort, a common scheduling/build scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn's algorithm)
- Longest path in a DAG via DP
- Cycle detection via processed-node count
- Handling multiple prerequisites and parallel tasks

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeEarliestFinish(n, durations, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = durations.slice();
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let processed = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (est[v] < est[u] + durations[v]) est[v] = est[u] + durations[v];
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (processed !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return { est, total: Math.max(...est) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the actual critical path from est and predecessors?
- How modify for weighted edges representing inter-task setup times instead of node durations?
- How to scale and detect cycles efficiently in streaming graphs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses dynamic programming fundamentals through practical string segmentation, testing understanding of both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Programming: memoization vs. tabulation
- String matching optimization (startsWith, trie)
- Time and space trade-offs between DP approaches
- State definition and transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function minWordSegmentsTopDown(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
  memo[n] = 0;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    let minSegs = Infinity;
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        const result = dfs(i + word.length);
        if (result !== -1) {
          minSegs = Math.min(minSegs, 1 + result);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[i] = minSegs === Infinity ? -1 : minSegs;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulated solution
function minWordSegmentsBottomUp(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[n] = 0;

  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        if (dp[i + word.length] !== Infinity) {
          dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], 1 + dp[i + word.length]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[0];
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time:** O(n × m × L) where n = |s|, m = dict size, L = avg word length. Using a Trie reduces this to O(n × L).
- **Space:** O(n) for the memo/DP array.

